BanMido wrote:
>
> Unfortunately, I haven't found an altar yet. But I do have my pet dog
> from level 1 with me, whom I use to check for BUC status, the "moves
> only reluctantly" hint. I am not sure how reliable that method is but
> in my experience, it has served me very well and so far, without
> exception.

It is completely accurate for telling cursed items.  It does not tell
uncursed items from blessed items.  You need an altar for that - Keep
exploring levels.

> I usually litter the items of potential interest around me,
> put my pet dog on a leash and wait several turns so that my dogs
> happily runs around me and I keep a eye on the "moves only
> reluctantly" messages. Is that a reasonable way to do it?

Absolutely.

> That brings out a question I had in mind, do cursed items *always*
> weld themselves to the wearer?

There are items types that aren't worn or wielded that don't do that,
but yes that's the main thing about cursed armor and the most obvious
disadvantage to cursed weapons.  The cursed ones tend to have negative
enchantment numbers - Higher positive numbers are better.

> If yes, then all the armor I have is
> certainly not cursed, as I have taken it off and put it on several
> times now but the description, still, does not say anything about its
> BUC status.

As long as you know armor is not cursed keep trying them on.  Armor that
gives a lower AC number is better, though AC is not the only
consideration with armor.

> Yes, the crystal plate mail is quite heavy. I found a +0 elven mithril
> coat but as it provided lesser protection, I left it out.

That's a matter of preferences and experience.  I tend to wear tested
mithril in spite of the weaker AC because it's less heavy.  For me the
price of heaviness beats the benefit of better AC.  He who runs away
lives to fight another day - In modern warfare that means the helicopter
gunship most often wins against the tank.

> Is there any way to improve the mithril coat so that I get
> to keep the same AC without extra burden? The guide book mentions
> something about enchanting items but I have no idea how that works.
> Could anyone give me a subtle hint/pointer in that direction? Or is
> that not not possible without massive/complete spoilage?

There are types of items other than weapons, armor and food.  How much
more information do you want becomes a matter of how spoiled you want to
get.

> Also, I am running out of arrows, at least the good ones, what do I do
> if I do not find a shop selling arrows soon? On a related note, can
> arrows be cursed, if yes how that affect me, if it does at all?

For someone a few years in the answers to those are not considered
spoilers.  To someone a month in may be a different matter.

It's good to see you are learning missile combat.  Few monsters are good
at missile combat and they generally come with few missiles and poor
armor so the player has a huge advantage using missile combat.  In
comparison fighting face to face with wielded weapons gives the monsters
as much chance to hit you as you get to hit them.  There are a lot more
monsters than your one player.


